## 4. DNS records to request

> **Who:** send this to your DNS administrator. Nothing to run on the nodes yet.

The cluster will be bound to the name `k8s-api.corp.example.com`. It must resolve before Step 3.

### Forward zone `corp.example.com` — A records

| Hostname | Type | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| k8s-api | A | 192.168.1.100 | Virtual IP, Kubernetes API endpoint |
| master1 | A | 192.168.1.11 | Control plane node |
| master2 | A | 192.168.1.12 | Control plane node |
| master3 | A | 192.168.1.13 | Control plane node |
| worker1 | A | 192.168.1.21 | Worker node |
| worker2 | A | 192.168.1.22 | Worker node |

### Reverse zone `1.168.192.in-addr.arpa` — PTR records

| Last octet | Type | Value |
|---|---|---|
| 100 | PTR | k8s-api.corp.example.com. |
| 11 | PTR | master1.corp.example.com. |
| 12 | PTR | master2.corp.example.com. |
| 13 | PTR | master3.corp.example.com. |
| 21 | PTR | worker1.corp.example.com. |
| 22 | PTR | worker2.corp.example.com. |

### Same records in BIND zone-file format

```
; forward zone: corp.example.com
k8s-api   IN  A    192.168.1.100
master1   IN  A    192.168.1.11
master2   IN  A    192.168.1.12
master3   IN  A    192.168.1.13
worker1   IN  A    192.168.1.21
worker2   IN  A    192.168.1.22

; reverse zone: 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa
100       IN  PTR  k8s-api.corp.example.com.
11        IN  PTR  master1.corp.example.com.
12        IN  PTR  master2.corp.example.com.
13        IN  PTR  master3.corp.example.com.
21        IN  PTR  worker1.corp.example.com.
22        IN  PTR  worker2.corp.example.com.
```

For Windows DNS: create each as "New Host (A or AAAA)" in the `corp.example.com` zone with "Create associated pointer (PTR) record" ticked. The reverse lookup zone `1.168.192.in-addr.arpa` must exist first.

Request a TTL of **300 seconds** on `k8s-api` so the VIP can be moved later without long cache delays.

**Why DNS instead of `/etc/hosts`?** A hosts file must be edited on every machine for every change, does not help pods inside the cluster, and does not help your workstation. Putting the API endpoint behind a DNS name also means you can move the load balancer to a different IP later without re-issuing cluster certificates.

### Verify (from any machine on the network)

```bash
dig k8s-api.corp.example.com +short     # expected: 192.168.1.100
dig -x 192.168.1.11 +short              # expected: master1.corp.example.com.
```

