## 5. Step 1 — Prepare all nodes

> **Run on:** master1, master2, master3, worker1, worker2 (every node)

### 1.1 Set the hostname

Change the name per node.

```bash
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname master1.corp.example.com
```

**Why:** The kubelet registers each node under its hostname, so names must be unique. Using the full FQDN makes the Kubernetes node name match DNS forward and reverse records, which avoids confusion in logs and certificate checks later.

### 1.2 Configure static IP, DNS servers and search domain

Find the netplan file name with `ls /etc/netplan/`, then write it (example for master1; change `addresses` per node):

```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/netplan/00-installer-config.yaml
network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    ens18:
      addresses: [192.168.1.11/24]
      routes:
        - to: default
          via: 192.168.1.1
      nameservers:
        addresses: [10.10.10.53, 10.10.10.54]
        search: [corp.example.com]
EOF
sudo netplan apply
```

Verify:

```bash
getent hosts k8s-api       # expected: 192.168.1.100 k8s-api.corp.example.com
hostname -f                # expected: master1.corp.example.com
ping -c1 google.com        # internet works
```

**Why:** Static IPs are mandatory. If DHCP ever changed a master's IP, etcd and the API server certificates would break. The `search` domain lets you type `k8s-api` and have it expand to the FQDN. If your organisation hands out static leases and DNS via DHCP, you can skip this file; just confirm the `getent` and `hostname -f` output.

### 1.3 Disable swap

```bash
sudo swapoff -a
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
```

**Why:** The kubelet enforces pod memory limits. With swap enabled, a pod can exceed its limit and spill to disk, which makes limits meaningless and performance unpredictable. The kubelet refuses to start if swap is on. The `sed` keeps swap off after reboot.

### 1.4 Kernel modules and sysctl settings

```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter

cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1
EOF
sudo sysctl --system
```

**Why:**
- `overlay` is the filesystem containerd uses to stack container image layers.
- `br_netfilter` and the `bridge-nf-call-*` settings make traffic crossing a Linux bridge visible to iptables, which kube-proxy (Services) and Calico (NetworkPolicy) depend on.
- `ip_forward` lets the node route packets between pods and the outside world. Without it each node is a dead end.

### 1.5 Install containerd (container runtime)

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml > /dev/null
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml
sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd
```

Verify:

```bash
sudo systemctl is-active containerd          # active
grep SystemdCgroup /etc/containerd/config.toml   # SystemdCgroup = true
```

**Why:** Kubernetes does not run containers itself; it instructs a runtime through the CRI interface. containerd is the lightweight standard. `SystemdCgroup = true` is critical: Ubuntu manages cgroups with systemd and so does the kubelet by default. If containerd used a different cgroup driver, two managers would fight over resource accounting and nodes would randomly go `NotReady`.

### 1.6 Install kubeadm, kubelet and kubectl

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.36/deb/Release.key | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.36/deb/ /' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo systemctl enable --now kubelet
```

Verify:

```bash
kubeadm version -o short     # same version on every node
```

**Why:**
- `kubeadm` bootstraps the cluster. `kubelet` is the agent on every node that starts and watches pods. `kubectl` is the command-line client.
- `apt-mark hold` stops a routine `apt upgrade` from changing the Kubernetes version. Kubernetes upgrades must be done deliberately, masters first, one minor version at a time.
- The kubelet will crash-loop until `kubeadm init` or `kubeadm join` gives it a configuration. That is expected.
- The `v1.36` in the repo URL selects the minor version line. All nodes must run the same version.

### 1.7 Firewall

For a lab you can simply disable it:

```bash
sudo ufw disable
```

For production, open only the required ports (see [Reference: ports](#14-reference-ports)):

```bash
# On masters:
sudo ufw allow 6443,8443,2379:2380,10250,10257,10259,179,5473/tcp
sudo ufw allow 4789/udp
sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 112 proto vrrp   # only if ufw blocks VRRP; see note

# On workers:
sudo ufw allow 10250,30000:32767,179,5473/tcp
sudo ufw allow 4789/udp
```

> Note: keepalived uses VRRP (IP protocol 112, multicast 224.0.0.18). Some firewalls block it. If the VIP appears on more than one master at once in Step 2, VRRP is being blocked between them.

**Why:** 179/tcp is Calico BGP (route distribution between nodes), 4789/udp is Calico VXLAN (pod traffic across subnets), 5473 is Calico Typha. If these are blocked, pods on different nodes cannot talk to each other even though everything shows `Running`.

