## 6. Step 2 — Load balancer on the three masters

> **Run on:** master1, master2, master3 (not on workers)

### 2.1 Install packages

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y haproxy keepalived
```

### 2.2 HAProxy configuration (identical on all three masters)

```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
global
    log /dev/log local0
    daemon

defaults
    mode tcp
    log global
    option tcplog
    timeout connect 10s
    timeout client  1m
    timeout server  1m

frontend k8s-api
    bind *:8443
    default_backend k8s-api

backend k8s-api
    balance roundrobin
    option httpchk GET /healthz
    http-check expect status 200
    server master1 192.168.1.11:6443 check check-ssl verify none
    server master2 192.168.1.12:6443 check check-ssl verify none
    server master3 192.168.1.13:6443 check check-ssl verify none
EOF
sudo systemctl restart haproxy && sudo systemctl enable haproxy
```

**Why:**
- Port **8443**: the Kubernetes API server already listens on 6443 on these same machines, so HAProxy needs a different port.
- `mode tcp`: the API server uses mutual TLS. HAProxy must pass the encrypted stream through untouched; it cannot terminate TLS.
- The `/healthz` check removes a dead API server from rotation within seconds.
- Backends are written as **IP addresses on purpose**. The load balancer keeps the API reachable, so it must never depend on DNS being up.

### 2.3 keepalived health check script (identical on all three masters)

```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/keepalived/check_apiserver.sh
#!/bin/sh
curl -sfk --max-time 2 https://localhost:8443/healthz -o /dev/null || exit 1
EOF
sudo chmod +x /etc/keepalived/check_apiserver.sh
```

**Why:** keepalived runs this every few seconds. If the local HAProxy stops answering, the node's priority is lowered and the VIP moves to another master.

### 2.4 keepalived configuration (differs per master)

Set `interface` to your NIC name (`ip a`). Set `state` and `priority` per node:

| Node | `state` | `priority` |
|---|---|---|
| master1 | MASTER | 101 |
| master2 | BACKUP | 100 |
| master3 | BACKUP | 99 |

```bash
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf
vrrp_script check_apiserver {
    script "/etc/keepalived/check_apiserver.sh"
    interval 3
    weight -2
    fall 10
    rise 2
}

vrrp_instance VI_1 {
    state MASTER
    interface ens18
    virtual_router_id 51
    priority 101
    authentication {
        auth_type PASS
        auth_pass k8s-vip
    }
    virtual_ipaddress {
        192.168.1.100
    }
    track_script {
        check_apiserver
    }
}
EOF
sudo systemctl restart keepalived && sudo systemctl enable keepalived
```

**Why:** VRRP elects the node with the highest priority to hold the VIP. When the health check fails, `weight -2` drops that node's priority below the next one and the VIP moves. `virtual_router_id` must be unique on your LAN if other keepalived clusters exist on the same network. `auth_pass` must be identical on all three.

### 2.5 Verify

```bash
# master1:
ip a | grep 192.168.1.100          # VIP present
# master2 and master3:
ip a | grep 192.168.1.100          # nothing (VIP is on master1)
```

Quick failover test of the load balancer alone:

```bash
# master1:
sudo systemctl stop keepalived
# master2:
ip a | grep 192.168.1.100          # VIP has moved here
# master1:
sudo systemctl start keepalived    # VIP returns to master1 (higher priority)
```

