## 7. Step 3 — Initialize the cluster on master1

> **Run on:** master1 ONLY

### 3.1 kubeadm init

```bash
sudo kubeadm init \
  --control-plane-endpoint "k8s-api.corp.example.com:8443" \
  --upload-certs \
  --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 \
  --apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.1.11
```

This takes 1 to 3 minutes.

**Why each flag:**
- `--control-plane-endpoint`: the single most important flag for HA. It puts the load balancer name into the API server certificate and every kubelet config. Without it the cluster is permanently tied to master1's IP and can never be made highly available.
- `--upload-certs`: encrypts the cluster CA certificates and stores them in a Kubernetes Secret, so the other masters can download them during join instead of you copying `/etc/kubernetes/pki` by hand.
- `--pod-network-cidr`: the IP range pods will use. Calico reads this in Step 4. It must not overlap any real network.
- `--apiserver-advertise-address`: binds the API server to the correct interface if the machine has more than one.

### 3.2 Save the join commands

The end of the output contains two commands. **Copy both into a safe place.** They look like:

```
You can now join any number of control-plane nodes running the following command on each as root:

  kubeadm join k8s-api.corp.example.com:8443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1234567890abcdef... \
    --control-plane --certificate-key f8902e114ef118304e561c3ecd4d0b543adc226b7a07f675f56564185ffe0c07

Then you can join any number of worker nodes by running the following on each as root:

  kubeadm join k8s-api.corp.example.com:8443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1234567890abcdef...
```

The **token** expires after 24 hours and the **certificate-key** after 2 hours. See [Troubleshooting](#13-troubleshooting) to regenerate them.

### 3.3 Configure kubectl for your user

```bash
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config
kubectl get nodes
```

Expected output:

```
NAME                        STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION
master1.corp.example.com    NotReady   control-plane   1m    v1.36.x
```

**Why `NotReady`:** the node is healthy but has no pod network yet. The kubelet reports `NotReady` until a CNI plugin is installed, and CoreDNS pods stay `Pending` for the same reason. Step 4 fixes this.

