## 8. Step 4 — Install Calico on master1

> **Run on:** master1 ONLY (kubectl commands; they apply cluster-wide)

### 4.1 Install the Tigera operator

```bash
CALICO=v3.32.2
kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/operator-crds.yaml
kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/tigera-operator.yaml
```

### 4.2 Configure the pod network CIDR and install Calico

```bash
curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/custom-resources.yaml
sed -i 's|192.168.0.0/16|10.244.0.0/16|' custom-resources.yaml
grep cidr custom-resources.yaml          # confirm: cidr: 10.244.0.0/16
kubectl create -f custom-resources.yaml
```

### 4.3 Wait and verify

```bash
watch kubectl get pods -n calico-system      # wait until all Running, then Ctrl+C
kubectl get tigerastatus                     # AVAILABLE = True for all
kubectl get nodes                            # master1 now Ready
kubectl get pods -n kube-system              # coredns pods Running
```

**Why:**
- Calico is installed through the **Tigera operator**, a controller that deploys Calico, keeps it healthy and handles upgrades. Order is: CRDs first, then the operator, then the `Installation` resource (`custom-resources.yaml`) that tells the operator what to deploy.
- The `sed` changes Calico's default pod CIDR (`192.168.0.0/16`) to match `--pod-network-cidr` from Step 3. Calico's default would collide with a `192.168.x.x` office network. If the two CIDRs do not match, pods get IPs the controller manager does not know about and networking breaks in confusing ways.
- `kubectl create` is used instead of `kubectl apply` because the operator manifest is too large for the annotation that `apply` adds.
- Calico runs a `calico-node` pod on every node automatically (it is a DaemonSet), so no per-node network configuration is needed when you add masters and workers later.

