## 9. Step 5 — Join master2 and master3

> **Run on:** master2 first. Wait until it is `Ready`. Then master3.

### 5.1 Join as a control-plane node

Use the **control-plane join command** saved in Step 3.2 and add `--apiserver-advertise-address` with that node's own IP:

```bash
# master2:
sudo kubeadm join k8s-api.corp.example.com:8443 --token <token> \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<hash> \
  --control-plane --certificate-key <key> \
  --apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.1.12

# master3 (after master2 is Ready):
sudo kubeadm join k8s-api.corp.example.com:8443 --token <token> \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<hash> \
  --control-plane --certificate-key <key> \
  --apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.1.13
```

### 5.2 Configure kubectl on each new master

```bash
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config
kubectl get nodes
```

**Why:**
- `--control-plane` tells kubeadm to run the API server, scheduler, controller manager and an **etcd member** on this node, not just a kubelet.
- `--certificate-key` decrypts the CA bundle uploaded in Step 3. Without it the node cannot become a control plane.
- `--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash` lets the joining node verify it is talking to the genuine cluster and not an impostor.
- Join masters **one at a time**. etcd adds members sequentially. Adding two at once can temporarily break quorum and fail the join.

