## 11. Step 7 — Verify the cluster

> **Run on:** any ONE master (master1 is fine)

### 7.1 Nodes and system pods

```bash
kubectl get nodes -o wide
```

Expected: 5 nodes, all `Ready`; masters show `ROLES = control-plane`.

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
```

Expected: three each of `etcd-*`, `kube-apiserver-*`, `kube-controller-manager-*`, `kube-scheduler-*`; `coredns-*` and `kube-proxy-*` all `Running`.

```bash
kubectl get pods -n calico-system -o wide
```

Expected: one `calico-node-*` per node (5 total), all `Running`.

### 7.2 etcd quorum

```bash
kubectl -n kube-system exec etcd-master1.corp.example.com -- etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  endpoint status --cluster -w table
```

Expected: 3 members, exactly one with `IS LEADER = true`.

**Why:** The API servers are stateless; etcd is what must survive a failure. Three members with a leader proves replication works.

### 7.3 Pod networking, Services and DNS

```bash
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=3
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --type=NodePort
kubectl get pods -o wide                     # pods spread across workers
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- sh -c \
  'wget -qO- nginx | head -3; nslookup k8s-api.corp.example.com'
```

Expected: HTML from nginx (proves pod-to-pod routing through Calico and Service resolution) and the VIP from `nslookup` (proves pods can reach your organisation's DNS through CoreDNS).

Clean up:

```bash
kubectl delete deployment nginx
kubectl delete service nginx
```

### 7.4 Control-plane failover test

```bash
# master1:
sudo poweroff

# master2:
ip a | grep 192.168.1.100        # VIP has moved here
kubectl get nodes                # still works; master1 shows NotReady
kubectl get pods -n kube-system  # etcd-master1 gone, cluster still serving
```

Power master1 back on. Within about a minute it rejoins and returns to `Ready`; the VIP moves back because master1 has the highest priority.

**Why:** This is the whole point of the design. If this test fails, do not put workloads on the cluster until it passes.

